WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.100
The voyage of the Parker Solar Probe

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.020
is about to begin.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.460
Its seven-year mission:

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.020
To boldly go where no craft has gone before.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.460
To touch the sun.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.900
To seek out answers to questions like:

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.780
Why is the sun’s corona so dang hot?

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.980
[music]

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.140
Smaller and speedier than starships past,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.980
NASA’s Parker Solar Probe is set to blast off

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.840
from Cape Canaveral aboard a Delta IV Heavy rocket —

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.840
the largest in NASA’s arsenal.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.600
After clearing Earth’s atmosphere,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.040
Parker will shed its rocket

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.740
and get one last boost.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.380
Then, spring-loaded gadgets

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.380
that were stowed for launch emerge.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.960
Solar panels pop out and start charging,

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.700
the cooling system revs up,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.700
and antennas and other instruments

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.020
stretch out into space.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.640
Six weeks after launch, Parker reaches Venus.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:59.880
It’ll use Venus’ gravity

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.480
to slingshot itself toward the sun.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.220
Another six weeks later,

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.760
Parker becomes the first spacecraft

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.180
to enter the sun’s outer atmosphere or corona.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.980
The corona is actually hotter

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.360
than the surface of the sun.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.520
But a pancake-shaped heat shield

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.580
keeps all of Parker’s gadgets

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.700
a little above room temperature.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.560
Most of the spacecraft’s solar panels also

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.980
tuck behind the carbon composite shield

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.540
to avoid melting.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.820
Traveling at high speeds will prevent Parker

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.520
from getting pulled into the sun.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.460
As the probe surfs the sun’s atmosphere,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.620
it’ll gather important intel on solar radiation,

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.900
electric fields, magnetic fields, and particles.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.620
The data could help explain

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.320
what heats up the corona

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.460
and how solar wind escapes the sun

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.340
and accelerates through the solar system.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.880
Then Parker swoops back out to cold, dark space

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.400
and transmits its data back to Earth.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.560
It’ll do the same thing 23 more times,

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.580
sweeping by the sun every 88 days.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:11.160
Venus will give the probe six more slingshot assists,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.500
and each time, it will inch closer

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.040
to the sun’s surface.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.520
While Parker is close to the sun,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.620
it won’t be able to radio home,

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.880
so the probe will have to fly solo.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:24.100
Tiny solar sensors tell the craft

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.740
to shimmy left or right

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.780
when they’re exposed to too much sun.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.900
Parts of Parker will get hit with

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.200
475 times the solar radiation that

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.600
spacecraft experience in orbit around Earth.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.900
The probe’s final three orbits will take it within

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.520
6.3 million kilometers of the sun’s surface.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.400
That’s seven times closer than

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.020
the current record holder, Helios 2 back in 1976.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:51.780
On its final orbit,

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.520
Parker will also come in fast and furious

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:58.660
at a top speed of 690,000 kilometers per hour

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.600
or 190 kilometers per second.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.500
It’ll be the fastest human-made object

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:05.920
in the solar system.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:07.480
At that speed,

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.600
you could travel from New York to Tokyo

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.060
in under a minute.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.180
So, top that, Vin Diesel.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.280
The mission is set for seven years,

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.980
but it could go longer.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.880
Whenever Parker meets its end,

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:23.880
it’ll go out in a blaze of glory.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.440
Once the probe runs out of fuel,

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.880
most of it will burn up

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.400
in the very star it was sent to study.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.320
But not the heat shield —

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.640
that giant carbon disk will continue to orbit the sun

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.040
for millions of years.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.720
[music]

